1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,844
Why branches exist?

2
00:00:07,400 --> 00:00:09,355
The problem branches solve:

3
00:00:09,355 --> 00:00:16,594
several pieces of work in progress at once, without stepping on each other or on the stable version.

4
00:00:16,594 --> 00:00:19,925
We feel the problem first, on a single branch.

5
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:26,066
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

6
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:30,535
Work on a new feature, directly on main, half finished:

7
00:00:30,535 --> 00:00:32,682
Now an urgent request arrives:

8
00:00:32,682 --> 00:00:36,974
the latte price is wrong and must be fixed and released now.

9
00:00:36,974 --> 00:00:39,980
But main contains a half-finished feature.

10
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:42,613
Now an urgent request arrives:

11
00:00:42,613 --> 00:00:46,772
the latte price is wrong and must be fixed and released now.

12
00:00:46,772 --> 00:00:49,683
But main contains a half-finished feature.

13
00:00:50,233 --> 00:00:53,992
Whatever we release from main now contains "WIP:

14
00:00:53,992 --> 00:00:55,088
loyalty card".

15
00:00:55,088 --> 00:00:58,534
The same work with a branch for the feature:

16
00:00:58,534 --> 00:01:01,196
main has the fix and only the fix:

17
00:01:01,196 --> 00:01:02,058
releasable.

18
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:05,281
Let me try something.

19
00:01:05,281 --> 00:01:07,397
Back in the single-branch lab:

20
00:01:07,397 --> 00:01:12,544
the release manager wants to ship the latte fix without the loyalty work.

21
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:14,708
The history is linear:

22
00:01:14,708 --> 00:01:17,558
Fix the latte price sits on top of WIP:

23
00:01:17,558 --> 00:01:18,508
loyalty card.

24
00:01:18,508 --> 00:01:23,624
Releasing the commit with the fix means releasing everything below it.

25
00:01:25,367 --> 00:01:28,016
Undo the mix-up the way a team would:

26
00:01:28,016 --> 00:01:31,094
move the unfinished work to its own branch,

27
00:01:31,094 --> 00:01:37,681
and rebuild main with only the fix (lesson 67 explains cherry-pick; here it copies the fix):

28
00:01:39,433 --> 00:01:40,147
Got it.

29
00:01:40,147 --> 00:01:42,898
What do I need to remember?

30
00:01:43,433 --> 00:01:47,853
One branch for everything means nothing is ever releasable on its own.

31
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:53,464
A branch per piece of work keeps main stable and lets work happen in parallel.

32
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,513
Branches are cheap:

33
00:01:55,513 --> 00:01:59,494
create them for every feature, fix and experiment.

34
00:02:01,233 --> 00:02:04,625
I keep hearing about creating branches.

35
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:06,278
What is it, really?

36
00:02:06,833 --> 00:02:09,925
git branch lists and creates branches.

37
00:02:09,925 --> 00:02:12,773
Creating one does not switch to it:

38
00:02:12,773 --> 00:02:14,238
a common surprise.

39
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,399
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

40
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:23,723
The * marks the current branch.

41
00:02:23,723 --> 00:02:25,163
Create a branch:

42
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:27,902
The marks the current branch.

43
00:02:27,902 --> 00:02:29,117
Create a branch:

44
00:02:29,117 --> 00:02:33,977
Two branches now point to the same commit, and is still on main:

45
00:02:33,977 --> 00:02:36,179
git branch NAME only creates.

46
00:02:36,733 --> 00:02:41,703
Two branches now point to the same commit, and * is still on main:

47
00:02:41,703 --> 00:02:43,887
git branch NAME only creates.

48
00:02:43,887 --> 00:02:47,200
The branch is a file containing a commit ID:

49
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:47,803
Same ID.

50
00:02:49,567 --> 00:02:51,345
Let me try something.

51
00:02:51,345 --> 00:02:53,122
Two classic mistakes:

52
00:02:53,122 --> 00:02:57,186
a name that already exists, and an invalid name.

53
00:02:57,733 --> 00:03:01,995
a branch named 'hotfix-prices' already exists:

54
00:03:01,995 --> 00:03:03,569
names are unique.

55
00:03:03,569 --> 00:03:07,275
'fix prices' is not a valid branch name:

56
00:03:07,275 --> 00:03:09,683
no spaces, no .., no ~ ^ :

57
00:03:09,683 --> 00:03:09,776
?

58
00:03:09,776 --> 00:03:11,906
* [ or ending in .lock.

59
00:03:11,906 --> 00:03:14,593
Check a name before using it:

60
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:18,133
Here is the fix.

61
00:03:19,867 --> 00:03:20,580
Got it.

62
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:23,331
What do I need to remember?

63
00:03:23,867 --> 00:03:28,906
git branch NAME creates a label at the current commit; it does not switch.

64
00:03:29,467 --> 00:03:34,396
git branch lists, -v adds the latest commit, -m renames.

65
00:03:34,933 --> 00:03:37,608
A branch is a file with a commit ID:

66
00:03:37,608 --> 00:03:41,398
creating one is instant, whatever the project size.

67
00:03:43,133 --> 00:03:45,728
How does switching branches work?

68
00:03:46,267 --> 00:03:51,034
git switch moves HEAD to another branch and updates your files to match it.

69
00:03:51,034 --> 00:03:57,136
We also meet the older git checkout, and what Git does with uncommitted changes when you switch.

70
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:03,299
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

71
00:04:03,833 --> 00:04:07,128
On main, the menu has three items:

72
00:04:07,667 --> 00:04:12,620
The file changed on disk, and HEAD now names feature-tea.

73
00:04:12,620 --> 00:04:13,576
Back again:

74
00:04:14,133 --> 00:04:18,527
The file changed on disk, and HEAD now names feature-tea.

75
00:04:18,527 --> 00:04:19,374
Back again:

76
00:04:19,374 --> 00:04:22,843
The older command does the same for branches:

77
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:26,086
Let me try something.

78
00:04:26,086 --> 00:04:31,039
Now an uncommitted change to a file that differs between the branches:

79
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:36,896
Your local changes to the following files would be overwritten by checkout:

80
00:04:36,896 --> 00:04:37,532
menu.txt.

81
00:04:37,532 --> 00:04:43,393
menu.txt is different on main; switching would have to replace your edited version,

82
00:04:43,393 --> 00:04:46,571
so Git refuses rather than destroy your work.

83
00:04:46,571 --> 00:04:49,889
You are still on feature-tea, nothing was lost:

84
00:04:51,633 --> 00:04:53,037
Three options:

85
00:04:53,037 --> 00:04:58,553
commit the change, stash it (lesson 35), or discard it.

86
00:04:58,553 --> 00:05:00,058
Commit it here:

87
00:05:01,800 --> 00:05:02,513
Got it.

88
00:05:02,513 --> 00:05:05,264
What do I need to remember?

89
00:05:05,800 --> 00:05:10,980
git switch NAME moves HEAD and updates your files; git switch - goes back.

90
00:05:11,533 --> 00:05:18,488
Uncommitted changes come along when they don't conflict; otherwise Git refuses, to protect your work.

91
00:05:19,033 --> 00:05:23,963
git checkout still works; git switch/git restore are clearer.

92
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:29,010
Why would I need create and switch in one command?

93
00:05:29,567 --> 00:05:33,177
git switch -c NAME creates a branch and switches to it:

94
00:05:33,177 --> 00:05:37,182
the command you will use to start almost every piece of work.

95
00:05:37,182 --> 00:05:39,611
And starting it from the right place.

96
00:05:41,367 --> 00:05:45,766
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

97
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:49,530
The new branch starts at the commit you were on.

98
00:05:49,530 --> 00:05:52,625
You can choose another starting point instead:

99
00:05:53,167 --> 00:05:56,295
The new branch starts at the commit you were on.

100
00:05:56,295 --> 00:05:59,292
You can choose another starting point instead:

101
00:05:59,292 --> 00:06:02,746
The older equivalent is git checkout -b NAME [START].

102
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:06,113
Let me try something.

103
00:06:06,113 --> 00:06:07,956
The most common mistake:

104
00:06:07,956 --> 00:06:11,335
starting a new branch from the wrong branch.

105
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:19,000
main..feature-specials should show only "Add specials", but it also contains "Add green tea to the menu":

106
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:23,463
the new branch inherited everything from the branch it started on.

107
00:06:23,463 --> 00:06:28,603
Always check where you are (git branch --show-current) before git switch -c,

108
00:06:28,603 --> 00:06:30,970
or name the start point explicitly.

109
00:06:32,733 --> 00:06:38,968
Rebuild the branch from main with only its own commit (lesson 61 explains rebase --onto):

110
00:06:40,733 --> 00:06:41,447
Got it.

111
00:06:41,447 --> 00:06:44,198
What do I need to remember?

112
00:06:44,733 --> 00:06:48,958
git switch -c NAME [START] creates and switches in one step.

113
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:53,145
A new branch contains everything of its starting point:

114
00:06:53,145 --> 00:06:54,935
start from the right place.

115
00:06:55,500 --> 00:07:01,130
--onto (lesson 61) repairs a branch started from the wrong place.

116
00:07:02,867 --> 00:07:06,116
What should I know about branch visualization?

117
00:07:06,667 --> 00:07:08,240
Watching branches move:

118
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:13,166
each commit moves the current branch's label forward, and only that one.

119
00:07:13,166 --> 00:07:18,296
We build a small history one commit at a time and draw it after every step.

120
00:07:20,033 --> 00:07:24,433
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

121
00:07:24,967 --> 00:07:28,617
Step 1, a branch at the same commit as main:

122
00:07:28,617 --> 00:07:30,940
Step 2, a commit on feature:

123
00:07:30,940 --> 00:07:33,346
only the feature label moves.

124
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:36,521
Step 2, a commit on feature:

125
00:07:36,521 --> 00:07:39,235
only the feature label moves.

126
00:07:39,235 --> 00:07:41,575
Step 3, a commit on main:

127
00:07:41,575 --> 00:07:43,635
now the history forks.

128
00:07:44,200 --> 00:07:46,726
Step 3, a commit on main:

129
00:07:46,726 --> 00:07:48,950
now the history forks.

130
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:52,235
Let me try something.

131
00:07:52,235 --> 00:07:54,208
Commit on the wrong branch:

132
00:07:54,208 --> 00:07:58,960
you meant to add the chai recipe to feature, but you are on main.

133
00:07:59,500 --> 00:08:02,962
The graph shows Add chai under the HEAD -> main label:

134
00:08:02,962 --> 00:08:05,783
the label that moved is the one HEAD was on.

135
00:08:05,783 --> 00:08:08,540
Committing always moves the current branch.

136
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:11,710
Move the commit:

137
00:08:11,710 --> 00:08:15,499
put it on feature (cherry-pick, lesson 67),

138
00:08:15,499 --> 00:08:22,725
then take it off main (reset, lesson 31; safe here because main was never pushed):

139
00:08:24,467 --> 00:08:25,180
Got it.

140
00:08:25,180 --> 00:08:27,931
What do I need to remember?

141
00:08:28,467 --> 00:08:31,656
A commit moves only the branch HEAD is on.

142
00:08:32,200 --> 00:08:38,305
Branches fork when two of them get different new commits; git merge-base finds the fork point.

143
00:08:38,867 --> 00:08:43,996
Draw the graph often; it answers most "where did my commit go" questions.

144
00:08:45,733 --> 00:08:48,453
Can you show me deleting branches?

145
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,977
How to delete branches safely with -d, why Git sometimes refuses,

146
00:08:53,977 --> 00:08:57,730
and when (and how carefully) to force it with -D.

147
00:08:59,467 --> 00:09:03,866
First, a fresh practice repository for this lesson.

148
00:09:04,400 --> 00:09:06,505
feature-tea is not merged yet.

149
00:09:06,505 --> 00:09:08,258
Merge it, then delete it:

150
00:09:08,258 --> 00:09:14,502
git branch --merged lists branches whose commits are all contained in the current branch:

151
00:09:14,502 --> 00:09:15,554
safe to delete.

152
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:19,324
Let me try something.

153
00:09:19,867 --> 00:09:20,276
error:

154
00:09:20,276 --> 00:09:23,276
the branch 'experiment' is not fully merged:

155
00:09:23,276 --> 00:09:31,800
experiment has a commit (Try oat milk) that no other branch contains; deleting the label would leave that commit unreachable.

156
00:09:31,800 --> 00:09:33,641
Look at what would be lost:

157
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:35,935
Decide.

158
00:09:35,935 --> 00:09:42,284
Either merge the work first, or confirm it is really unwanted and force the delete.

159
00:09:44,033 --> 00:09:44,747
Got it.

160
00:09:44,747 --> 00:09:47,498
What do I need to remember?

161
00:09:48,033 --> 00:09:51,943
-d deletes only merged branches; -D forces.

162
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:57,205
Deleting a branch deletes a label; merged commits stay in history.

163
00:09:57,767 --> 00:10:00,942
Before -D, look at git log main..BRANCH:

164
00:10:00,942 --> 00:10:04,356
that is exactly what you are throwing away.
